from pt import *
items = [
 HDR(),
 T(r"""and GoTube (Gruenbacher et al., 2022). We implement GoTube using the $\epsilon$-RandUP algorithm where we replace the last convex hull bounding step with an outer-bounding ball. As ground-truth, we use the reachable sets from $\epsilon$-RandUP with $\epsilon=0$ and $M=10^6$, which is motivated by the asymptotic results from Theorem 1 and was previously done in (Everett et al., 2021). We refer to Appendix E.2 for details and present results in Figures 4 and 5.""", [90, 93, 523, 159], join_previous="space"),
 F("Figure 5", "figure-5", [349, 166, 514, 369]),
 C(r"""Figure 5: Neural network verification analysis in Section 6.2: we report the computation time of each algorithm and their averaged Hausdorff distance error (with $\epsilon=0$ for $\epsilon$-RandUP and GoTube) over $100$ tries when estimating $\mathcal{Y}=\mathcal{X}_4$.""", [349, 370, 516, 440]),
 T(r"""**Formal methods** that explicitly bound the output of each layer of the neural network can guarantee that their reachable set approximations are always conservative. However, obtaining tight approximations with ReachLP requires splitting the input set: a computationally expensive procedure (Fig. 5, bottom). Figures 4 and 5 show that ReachLP is more conservative than $\epsilon$-RandUP even when considering polytopic outputs with eight facets. As shown in Figure 4 (right), the conservatism of these methods increases over time. This shows that even when considering small neural networks, verifying safety specifications over long horizons remains an open challenge.""", [90, 161, 340, 322]),
 T(r"""**Sampling-based** approaches do not suffer from the long-horizon conservatism of formal methods. This comes at the expense of probabilistic guarantees (that rely on knowledge of the Lipschitz constant of the model), as opposed to deterministic conservatism guarantees. $\epsilon$-RandUP and GoTube have comparable computation time $^{2}$ and are significantly faster than other approaches. $\epsilon$-RandUP is significantly more accurate than prior work, especially for larger values of $M$. Also, the results from Theorem 2 allow for principled hyperparameter selection for $\epsilon$-RandUP: given $\epsilon=0.02$, sampling $1400$ uniformly-distributed inputs on $\partial\mathcal{X}$ is sufficient for the output sets to be conservative with probability at least $1-10^{-4}$ (for $L=1$, see Section E.2).""", [90, 323, 523, 483]),
 T(r"""These experiments show that for short-horizon problems ($5$ steps) with relatively simple network architectures, both ReachLP and $\epsilon$-RandUP return accurate reachable set approximations. For longer-horizon problems ($9$ steps) with networks of moderate dimensions (which allows using existing methods to pre-compute a Lipschitz constant, see (Fazlyab et al., 2019) and Section D), $\epsilon$-RandUP is guaranteed to efficiently return non-overly-conservative reachable set approximations with high probability. Finally, though we do not present such results here, the generality of $\epsilon$-RandUP allows it to tackle complex model architectures (see (Lew et al., 2022) for experiments with longer horizons and more complex networks with uncertain weights) for which no alternative methods exist, albeit without finite-sample accuracy guarantees.""", [90, 485, 523, 606]),
 T(r"""Footnote 2: Plotting the kernel-based level set estimator in (Thorpe et al., 2021) from $M$ samples requires classifying a dense grid of points. To evaluate the computation time of this method, we only account for the time to classify $M$ new samples.""", [90, 673, 523, 696]),
 H(r"### 6.3. Application to robust model predictive control", [90, 619, 328, 631]),
 T(r"""Finally, we show that $\epsilon$-RandUP can be embedded in a robust MPC formulation to reliably control a planar spacecraft system actuated by cold-gas thrusters. Its state at time $t\geq 0$ is denoted as $x_t\in\mathbb{R}^6$""", [90, 638, 523, 665]),
 PNUM(9),
]
page(9, items, r"""Text taken from the authors' TeX (main.tex lines 1074-1208), macros expanded (\gotube small caps -> 'GoTube', \reachlp -> 'ReachLP'), and compared with the 150-dpi render. First item continues the sentence from page 8 ('...(Thorpe et al., 2021)' / 'and GoTube ...'): join_previous=space. Figure 5 (wrapped at the right, two stacked log-log panels: Hausdorff error d_H(\hat Y^M, Y) and Time [s] versus M) cropped to [349,166,514,369]; checked on a 200-dpi crop that the y labels, tick labels 10^0...10^-3 and 10^2...10^4, the in-plot annotations (ReachLP NoSplit / ReachLP Split, rectangular output / polytopic output), the legend (RandUP, Kernel, GoTube) and the x label M are inside and the caption is outside. Figure and caption placed after the first (page-continuing) paragraph and before the two paragraphs printed beside it. Numbers checked against the page: eps = 0 and M = 10^6 for the ground truth; eps = 0.02, 1400 samples, probability at least 1 - 10^{-4}, L = 1; 5 steps / 9 steps; 100 tries, Y = X_4 in the caption. Cross-references as printed: 'Appendix E.2', 'Section E.2', 'Section D', 'Fig. 5, bottom', 'Figure 4 (right)'. Line-wrap hyphens removed ('expen-sive', 'meth-ods', 'con-sidering', 'specifi-cations', 'net-work', 'us-ing', 'approxi-mations', 'conserva-tive'); real compounds kept ('long-horizon', 'short-horizon', 'longer-horizon', 'non-overly-conservative', 'pre-compute', 'cold-gas', 'uniformly-distributed'). Footnote mark 2 kept after 'computation time' (written '$^{2}$'). Footnote 2 is printed at the bottom of the page, below the first lines of Section 6.3 whose sentence continues on page 10; to keep that sentence unbroken the footnote item is placed at the end of Section 6.2 (after the paragraph 'These experiments show ...', before the 6.3 heading), i.e. right after the section that carries its mark. The page break falls inside the formula 'x_t in R^6' ('x_t in' is the last glyph group of this page, 'R^6' the first of page 10); the whole formula $x_t\in\mathbb{R}^6$ is written on this page and page 10 continues with 'and its control inputs ...'. Heading fixed to '### 6.3. ...'. Running header and page number 9 omitted.""")
